{"name":"Cocktail Onion","hex":"#dce2ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cocktail onion","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pearl onion or other sweet onion seasoned and pickled in brine, used as a cocktail garnish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cocktail_onion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cocktail onion","blocks":[{"text":"A cocktail onion is usually a pearl onion pickled in a brine with small amounts of turmeric and paprika. Pearl onions are usually chosen for their natural sweetness, but other sweet onions such as the crystal wax, also known as the white Bermuda, are also sometimes used. In many cases, white varieties of these sweet onions are used, since many consumers expect cocktail onions to be white. However, yellow or red sweet onions may be used as well. In northern California cuisine some haute bars may use sliced red onion pickled in vinegar. Some recipes also call for the onions to be packed in white vermouth instead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Cocktail_onions.JPG/330px-Cocktail_onions.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cocktail_onion","wikidata":"Q5139704"},{"title":"Cocktail","blocks":[{"text":"A cocktail is a mixed drink, usually alcoholic. Most commonly, a cocktail is a combination of one or more spirits mixed with other ingredients, such as juices, flavored syrups, tonic water, shrubs, and bitters. Cocktails vary widely across regions of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0084.jpg/330px-15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0084.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cocktail","wikidata":"Q134768"},{"title":"Onion","blocks":[{"text":"The onion, also known as the bulb onion or common onion, is a vegetable that is the most widely cultivated species of the genus Allium. The shallot is a botanical variety of the onion which was classified as a separate species until 2011. The onion's close relatives include garlic, scallion, leek, and chives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mixed_onions.jpg/330px-Mixed_onions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onion","wikidata":"Q23485"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Gibson_cocktail.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Gibson_cocktail.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Gibson_cocktail.tif/lossless-page1-1280px-Gibson_cocktail.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Gibson_cocktail.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Gibson_cocktail.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gibson cocktail using green cocktail onion","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGibson_cocktail.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg/500px-Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg/500px-Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cocktail accessories dating back to the 4th century BCE. Museum of the Royal Tombs of Aigai, Greece","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/The_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg/500px-The_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thum